-<h2 id="ancient-mesopotamia">Dream Interpretation in Ancient Mesopotamia: The First Dream Interpreters</h2>
-<p>
-                    The earliest written records of dream interpretation come from ancient <strong>Mesopotamia around 3100 BCE</strong>. The Sumerians, Assyrians, and Babylonians kept meticulous dream records on clay tablets, considering dreams to be messages from the divine realm.
-                </p>
-<p>
-                    Best known among these records is the <strong>Assyrian Dream Book</strong>, a collection of dream omens dating to the 7th century BCE. This text categorized dreams and their meanings with remarkable specificity:
-                </p>
-<ul>
-<li><strong>Flying dreams:</strong> Indicated freedom from burdens or elevation in status</li>
-<li><strong><a class="text-dream-salmon hover:underline" href="../symbols/falling">Falling dreams</a>:</strong> Warned of loss of position or divine favor</li>
-<li><strong>Water dreams:</strong> Related to purification, life force, or emotional states</li>
-<li><strong>Animal appearances:</strong> Each creature held symbolic significance tied to deities</li>
-</ul>
-<p>
-                    In Mesopotamian culture, dreams were considered a <strong>direct communication channel with gods</strong>. Kings would sleep in temples specifically to receive divine guidance through dreams - a practice called "incubation dreaming."
-                </p>
-<blockquote>
-                    "If a man flies repeatedly in his dreams, whatever he owns will be lost." - Assyrian Dream Book, circa 7th century BCE
-                </blockquote>
-<h2 id="ancient-egypt">Dream Interpretation in Ancient Egypt: Dreams as Divine Messages</h2>
-<p>
-                    Ancient Egyptians elevated dream interpretation to a sophisticated art form. By <strong>2000 BCE</strong>, they had established a professional class of dream interpreters who served in temples and advised pharaohs.
-                </p>
-<p>
-                    The <strong>Chester Beatty Papyrus III</strong>, dating to approximately 1350 BCE, is one of the oldest surviving dream interpretation manuals. This text contains over 200 dream scenarios and their meanings, organized systematically:
-                </p>
-<h3>Egyptian Dreams Considered Favorable (White Ink):</h3>
-<ul>
-<li>Seeing oneself dead: portended a long life</li>
-<li>Drinking beer: indicated spiritual nourishment</li>
-<li>Seeing the moon shining: meant forgiveness from the gods</li>
-</ul>
-<h3>Egyptian Dreams Considered Unfavorable (Red Ink):</h3>
-<ul>
-<li>Looking into a deep well: signified imprisonment</li>
-<li>Seeing one's face in a mirror: warned of a second wife</li>
-<li>Uncovering one's own backside: predicted orphanhood</li>
-</ul>
-<p>
-                    Egyptians believed dreams could be influenced through <strong>magical rituals and incantations</strong>. They would recite spells before sleep to encourage prophetic dreams or ward off <a class="text-dream-salmon hover:underline" href="stop-nightmares-guide">nightmares</a>, often involving the god Bes, the protector of sleep.
-                </p>
-<h2 id="ancient-greece">Dream Interpretation in Ancient Greece and Rome: Medical and Prophetic Dreams</h2>
-<p>
-                    Ancient Greeks approached dreams from both <strong>medical and mystical perspectives</strong>. By the 5th century BCE, Greek physicians began treating dreams as diagnostic tools for physical illness.
-                </p>
-<p>
-<strong>Hippocrates</strong> (460-370 BCE), the father of medicine, wrote that dreams could reveal imbalances in bodily humors. He believed certain dream images indicated specific health conditions - for example, dreams of <a class="text-dream-salmon hover:underline" href="../symbols/flood">floods</a> might suggest excess phlegm.
-                </p>
-<p>
-                    <strong>Aristotle</strong> (384-322 BCE) pushed back with a rationalist view, arguing in "On Dreams" that dreams were not divine messages but mental residues of daily experiences and sensory impressions processed during sleep.
-                </p>
-<p>
-                    Yet the most influential Greek text proved to be <strong>Artemidorus's "Oneirocritica"</strong> (The Interpretation of Dreams), written around 200 CE. This five-volume work became the standard dream interpretation manual for over 1,500 years. Artemidorus classified dreams into two categories:
-                </p>
-<ul>
-<li><strong>Theorematic dreams:</strong> Direct prophecies that would literally come true</li>
-<li><strong>Allegorical dreams:</strong> Symbolic visions requiring interpretation</li>
-</ul>
-<p>
-                    Greek and Roman cultures also practiced <strong>temple sleep</strong> at healing sanctuaries dedicated to Asclepius, the god of medicine. Sick individuals would sleep in sacred chambers, hoping to receive healing dreams or medical guidance directly from the deity.
-                </p>
-<h2 id="religious-traditions">Religious Dream Interpretation Across Cultures</h2>
-<p>
-                    Dreams hold profound significance in virtually all major religious traditions, often viewed as a channel for divine revelation.
-                </p>
-<h3>Dream Interpretation in Judaism and Christianity</h3>
-<p>
-                    The Hebrew Bible contains over 20 significant dream narratives. <strong>Joseph's interpretation of Pharaoh's dreams</strong> (Genesis 41) established a model for prophetic dream reading. The Talmud later declared, "A dream uninterpreted is like a letter unread."
-                </p>
-<p>
-                    Within Christianity, dreams guided crucial events: Joseph's dream about Mary's <a class="text-dream-salmon hover:underline" href="../symbols/pregnancy">pregnancy</a>, the Magi's warning not to return to Herod, and numerous visions in the Book of Revelation. Medieval Christian scholars debated whether dreams came from God, nature, or demons.
-                </p>
-<h3>Dream Interpretation in Islamic Tradition</h3>
-<p>
-                    Islamic tradition recognizes <strong>three types of dreams</strong>: true dreams from Allah (ru'ya), dreams from one's own thoughts (hulm), and dreams from Satan. The Prophet Muhammad reportedly said that true dreams are "one of the forty-six parts of prophethood."
-                </p>
-<p>
-                    Scholar <strong>Ibn Sirin</strong> (653-728 CE) compiled one of Islam's most important dream interpretation manuals, still influential today. His interpretations emphasized the dreamer's personal context and spiritual state.
-                </p>
-<h3>Dream Interpretation in Hinduism and Buddhism</h3>
-<p>
-                    In Hindu tradition, dreams are discussed in ancient texts like the <strong>Upanishads</strong>, which describe dreams as a state of consciousness between waking and deep sleep. The Mandukya Upanishad outlines four states of consciousness, with dreams representing an intermediate realm.
-                </p>
-<p>
-                    Buddhist texts describe dreams as <strong>illusory experiences</strong> that demonstrate the mind's nature. Tibetan Buddhism developed sophisticated practices of dream yoga, using <a class="text-dream-salmon hover:underline" href="lucid-dreaming-beginners-guide">lucid dreaming</a> as a path to spiritual awakening and understanding the nature of reality.
-                </p>
-<h2 id="medieval-period">Dream Interpretation in the Medieval and Renaissance Periods</h2>
-<p>
-                    During the <strong>Middle Ages (500-1500 CE)</strong>, European dream interpretation was dominated by Christian theology. Dreams were categorized as divine revelations, natural occurrences from bodily humors, or demonic deceptions.
-                </p>
-<p>
-<strong>Thomas Aquinas</strong> (1225-1274) synthesized Aristotelian philosophy with Christian doctrine, arguing that while most dreams result from natural causes, God could occasionally use dreams to communicate with humans.
-                </p>
-<p>
-                    The Renaissance brought renewed interest in classical texts, particularly Artemidorus's work. Dream interpretation manuals became popular publications:
-                </p>
-<ul>
-<li><strong>Macrobius's "Commentary on the Dream of Scipio"</strong> (5th century) was widely studied</li>
-<li><strong>Girolamo Cardano</strong> (1501-1576) wrote extensively on dream divination</li>
-<li>Popular almanacs included dream dictionaries for common folk</li>
-</ul>
-<p>
-                    Then the <strong>Age of Enlightenment</strong> (1685-1815) brought skepticism. Rationalist philosophers dismissed dream interpretation as superstition, viewing dreams as meaningless mental noise.
-                </p>
+<h2 id="ancient-mesopotamia">Dream Interpretation in Ancient Mesopotamia: A Documented Dream</h2>
+<p>The <a class="text-dream-salmon hover:underline" href="https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010124631" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Louvre dates Cylinder A of Gudea</a> to 2120–2110 BCE. Its inscription describes the god Ningirsu appearing to Gudea in a dream and directing the construction of a temple. The narrative connects the ruler's building project with divine authority.</p>
+<p>This is a specific surviving example of the religious role of dreams in ancient Mesopotamia. It does not tell us who first interpreted a dream: the date of an object is different from the beginning of a human practice.</p>
+<h2 id="ancient-egypt">Dream Interpretation in Ancient Egypt: A Written Manual</h2>
+<p><a class="text-dream-salmon hover:underline" href="https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection/object/Y_EA10683-3" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Papyrus Chester Beatty III in the British Museum</a> includes a dream book written in hieratic script. It offers another kind of evidence: a manuscript devoted to interpreting dreams, rather than a royal account of one dream.</p>
+<p>Reading such texts helps us understand a historical practice. Their survival does not validate the predictions they contain, and a single manuscript cannot represent everything ancient Egyptians believed about dreaming.</p>
+<h2 id="ancient-greece">Ancient Greece: Natural Explanations of Dreams</h2>
+<p>In <a class="text-dream-salmon hover:underline" href="https://classics.mit.edu/Aristotle/dreams.html" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><em>On Dreams</em></a>, Aristotle examines dreaming through perception and imagination. He considers how sensory impressions can persist when their original objects are no longer present. This is an attempt to explain a mental experience through natural processes, many centuries before psychoanalysis.</p>
+<p>His separate work <a class="text-dream-salmon hover:underline" href="https://classics.mit.edu/Aristotle/prophesying.html" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><em>On Prophesying by Dreams</em></a> addresses prediction. It considers bodily signs, actions prompted by a dream and coincidences. This discussion connects dreams with questions about health as well as divination; it is not a modern diagnostic method.</p>
+<p>These texts complicate a simple story in which all ancient people saw dreams as supernatural messages. They also show why explanations of how dreams arise should be distinguished from claims about what a particular dream predicts.</p>
+<h2 id="religious-traditions">A Religious Dream Narrative: Joseph and Pharaoh</h2>
+<p>In <a class="text-dream-salmon hover:underline" href="https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Genesis%2041&amp;version=KJV" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Genesis 41</a>, Joseph interprets Pharaoh's dreams of cattle and grain as seven years of abundance followed by seven years of famine. Within the narrative, the interpretation is attributed to God and leads to a plan for storing food.</p>
+<p>This passage provides a concrete example of dream interpretation in a religious text. It should be read as that text's account, rather than as experimental evidence for prediction or a summary of every Jewish or Christian view.</p>
+<h2 id="medieval-period">Medieval Perspectives: Aquinas on the Causes of Dreams</h2>
+<p>In <a class="text-dream-salmon hover:underline" href="https://www.newadvent.org/summa/3095.htm#article6" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"><em>Summa Theologiae</em>, question 95, article 6</a>, Thomas Aquinas asks whether divination through dreams is unlawful. His answer distinguishes natural causes, including bodily conditions and waking thoughts, from supernatural causes within his theological framework.</p>
+<p>The passage shows natural and religious explanations appearing together in one medieval argument. Treating the whole period as either unquestioning belief or rejection of dreams would obscure that distinction.</p>
