-<h2 id="cultural-differences">Cultural Differences in Dream Interpretation Around the World</h2>
-<p>
-                    Western psychology has dominated academic dream research, yet cultures worldwide maintain <strong>vastly different approaches</strong> to understanding dreams. These differences reveal fundamental variations in worldviews.
-                </p>
-<h3>Western Individualistic Approach to Dream Interpretation</h3>
-<p>
-                    Western psychology, following Freud and Jung, focuses on dreams as expressions of <strong>individual psychology</strong> - personal memories, desires, conflicts, and development. The dreamer is typically the sole author and subject of their dreams.
-                </p>
-<h3>Dream Interpretation in Indigenous and Tribal Traditions</h3>
-<p>
-                    Many indigenous cultures view dreams as <strong>collective experiences with social significance</strong>. Among the Iroquois of North America, important dreams were shared with the entire community, and the tribe would work together to fulfill the dream's message.
-                </p>
-<p>
-                    Australian Aboriginal cultures see dreams as access to the <strong>"Dreamtime"</strong> - a timeless dimension where ancestral beings shaped the world. Dreams connect individuals to land, ancestors, and cosmic law.
-                </p>
-<p>
-                    The Achuar people of Ecuador practice <strong>daily dream-sharing</strong> each morning. Dreams guide hunting decisions, social relations, and community direction. Shamans receive special training in dream interpretation and navigation.
-                </p>
-<h3>East Asian Perspectives on Dream Interpretation</h3>
-<p>
-                    Traditional Chinese medicine interprets dreams through the lens of <strong>energy systems and organ health</strong>. Different organs are associated with specific emotions and dream types:
-                </p>
-<ul>
-<li><strong>Liver imbalance:</strong> Dreams of anger, forests, or being lost</li>
-<li><strong>Heart imbalance:</strong> Dreams of <a class="text-dream-salmon hover:underline" href="../symbols/fire">fire</a>, laughter, or anxiety</li>
-<li><strong>Kidney imbalance:</strong> Dreams of <a class="text-dream-salmon hover:underline" href="../symbols/water">water</a>, fear, or <a class="text-dream-salmon hover:underline" href="../symbols/falling">falling</a></li>
-</ul>
-<p>
-                    In Japanese culture, the concept of <strong>"yūgen"</strong> (profound mystery) extends to dreams, which are seen as aesthetic experiences revealing deeper truths beyond rational understanding.
-                </p>
-<h3>Modern Multicultural Synthesis of Dream Interpretation</h3>
-<p>
-                    Contemporary dream workers increasingly recognize that <strong>interpretation methods should honor cultural context</strong>. A <a class="text-dream-salmon hover:underline" href="../symbols/snake">snake</a> in a dream might represent wisdom in Hindu tradition, danger in Western contexts, or healing in Native American perspectives.
-                </p>
-<p>
-                    This awareness has enriched Western psychology, producing more nuanced, context-sensitive approaches that acknowledge both universal patterns and cultural specificity.
-                </p>
-<h3>The Future of Dream Interpretation and Science</h3>
-<p>
-                    As neuroscience advances, we're developing tools that seemed like science fiction: <strong>decoding dream content from brain scans, inducing specific dream themes, and even communicating with lucid dreamers during sleep</strong>.
-                </p>
-<p>
-                    Yet the fundamental human impulse to find meaning in dreams persists. Perhaps the most profound insight from this 5,000-year history is that dreams serve multiple functions: they process emotions, consolidate memories, simulate threats, express desires, and reflect our deepest concerns - all simultaneously.
-                </p>
-<p>
-                    What this 5,000-year history reveals is an evolving conversation between humans and their inner worlds, each era contributing unique insights while building on ancient wisdom.
-                </p>
+<h2 id="cultural-differences">Reading Dream Beliefs in Their Cultural Context</h2>
+<p>A single account cannot represent every member of a religion, region or community. When reading an interpretation, ask who recorded it, when, and whether it describes a belief, a personal practice or a research finding. Avoid assigning one meaning to an image simply because it is associated with a broad cultural label.</p>
+<p>The history of dream interpretation brings together different ways of asking what dreams mean. Ancient texts document practices; psychological theories offer interpretive frameworks; sleep experiments investigate measurable aspects of dreaming. Keeping those forms of evidence distinct makes the history more useful without turning any one approach into a universal code.</p>
